WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.720
Christopher Riley, Screenwriter,&nbsp;
Author, and Instructor: You find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.160
all of these but they're like you&nbsp;
know their cards in your poker hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.760
and you're not sure where you're going to play&nbsp;
them or even if you're going to play them.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:17.200
Film Courage: When we're developing a character,&nbsp;
how much are we charting out all of sort of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.320
wonderful and terrible things that have happened&nbsp;
to them? Are we doing this beforehand before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:28.320
we get into the actual story, we're kind of&nbsp;
coming up with a checklist of defining moments?

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:41.120
Christopher: This is one of the confounding&nbsp;
qualities of storytelling. We don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.320
a linear process where we do this&nbsp;
first and the second. We sort of try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:55.280
we have to have we have to approach the story&nbsp;
somehow but I think that what we do is we

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:03.200
I typically have a notion for what&nbsp;
is the story what maybe in a sentence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:10.400
what is the story I want to tell and that&nbsp;
tells me something about a character and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.120
and also something about the plot&nbsp;
that is it's somebody like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:27.920
trying to you know climb out of a well and so but&nbsp;
then I go back to the character and I said well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.600
I have more questions for you character where did&nbsp;
you come from how did you get in that well what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:42.640
were you doing in the field in the first place and&nbsp;
sometimes the answers are really surprising to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:52.160
sometimes they come as sort of epiphanies as I'm&nbsp;
out for a run or taking a shower or lying in bed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.640
often though they come as a result of&nbsp;
just the ditch digging of sitting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:08.080
asking these questions I know that this&nbsp;
person to be interesting needs to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.800
both strength and weakness when did&nbsp;
they discover they had that strength

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:24.480
when did they first experience the&nbsp;
crippling quality of that weakness and um

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.920
then I come bring the character back to the story&nbsp;
and now they're doing more interesting things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.440
that I didn't expect them to do because there's&nbsp;
more to that character and so I discover more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:44.640
twists and turns that are potential within&nbsp;
say the events or the plot of the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:54.080
and then I go back to the character so I'm I&nbsp;
think I'm it's a very messy recursive process

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.440
when I am finding these defining moments&nbsp;
for a character I don't know if any of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.880
will end up on screen at first I'm&nbsp;
just discovering what are these moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:14.320
and then so there are things I can do with&nbsp;
a defining moment I can just know about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:20.160
and it's going to inform how I write dialogue&nbsp;
for this character other times I'm going to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:29.600
if I were writing Pixar's Finding Nemo I would&nbsp;
say oh that moment when marlin's whole family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:36.960
gets eaten by a barracuda I think we should see&nbsp;
that that's really visual and exciting and tragic

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:48.640
would Pixar allow us to start a children's movie&nbsp;
with that kind of scene because if we were to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:04:00.080
that then we would really understand why Marlon is&nbsp;
this overprotective father who won't let Nemo have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:08.560
any adventure have any fun experience any danger&nbsp;
and we won't view him just as you know oh that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:16.480
neurotic marlin we will feel with him well of&nbsp;
course he is a hyper vigilant parent because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.320
this tragedy struck him and we&nbsp;
witnessed it we experienced it with him

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.840
now when I first came up with that I didn't&nbsp;
somebody did if I was the one who had come up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:35.520
with that I i wouldn't have known at first&nbsp;
does that make it into the movie or not

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:45.200
there are other moments that we don't see but a&nbsp;
character talks about they just tell you the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:52.160
of what happened or they allude to the&nbsp;
story whatever makes great dialogue and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:59.200
other times there are moments right in the middle&nbsp;
of the movie where a character transforms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:07.200
here I think the great example is Michael Corleone&nbsp;
and the godfather so we know that Michael is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:16.000
war hero just back from world war ii he's the&nbsp;
son who was not supposed to get mixed up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.680
the family's crime business he was supposed to go&nbsp;
on and maybe be a senator maybe even a president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:30.320
but after his father gets gunned down Michael&nbsp;
decides he's got to take matters into his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:40.800
hands and so he goes to meet the crime boss who&nbsp;
had his father gunned down and the police captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:47.360
who protects that other crime boss because to meet&nbsp;
them at an Italian restaurant he has a gun planted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:54.560
in the bathroom and he excuses himself from the&nbsp;
table goes back to the bathroom gets the gun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:07.680
and then there's this long pause as Michael&nbsp;
smoothes his hair and sort of allows himself to

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:18.080
make his choice am I going to step across this&nbsp;
threshold from which there can be no return or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:25.920
and then the director has set it up so Michael&nbsp;
has to pass through three sets of doors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.560
as he returns into the&nbsp;
restaurant from the bathroom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:38.480
just to emphasize visually this is a threshold&nbsp;
there is a real boundary of before and after here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:45.440
Michael walks out into the restaurant guns down&nbsp;
the police captain and the other crime boss and

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:54.720
the rest of the movie and the next movie and&nbsp;
the next movie the die is cast for all of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:02.960
in that moment of the change of Michael's&nbsp;
legal status from war hero to killer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:09.920
there's also a moral and a spiritual&nbsp;
change that happens in him and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.840
he thinks he's done this for&nbsp;
all the best reasons for family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:26.640
but in the end he becomes someone who kills&nbsp;
members of his family for the family and he is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:36.960
undone by this action by this choice that he's&nbsp;
made in a moment that defines him so you find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:41.520
that moment and you think ah that's probably&nbsp;
a scene in the movie and in fact that becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:52.080
the fulcr of the whole movie the moment that&nbsp;
defines Michael as who will become the godfather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.600 --> 00:08:01.040
so you you find all of these but they're like you&nbsp;
know their cards in your poker hand and you're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:06.720
sure where you're going to play them or even if&nbsp;
you're going to play them and that is the messy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:15.120
organic artistic process I suppose of crafting&nbsp;
a story but you can't craft it if you don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:22.720
the raw materials from which to craft it and&nbsp;
I think that defining moments are one of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:34.880
really rich materials that we have to work with&nbsp;
when we're telling stories about characters.

